<section class="page-hero"><img class="bg" src="/assets/img/photos/tree-crew.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/tree-crew-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/tree-crew.webp 1200w" sizes="100vw" width="1200" height="800" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.1"><div class="wrap"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/claim-dispute-services/">Services</a><span>/</span>Tree Fall &amp; Ice Storm Appraisal</nav><p class="kicker lt">Claim dispute service</p><h1>Tree Fall Claim Appraisal in SC</h1><p class="lede">South Carolina’s pine and live-oak canopy is beautiful until it lands on a roof. Tree claims combine removal limits, structural repairs and interior water damage, and each piece is easy to underprice.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn cu" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn ol" href="/contact-an-appraiser/">Free claim review</a></div><div class="facts"><div><small>Best for</small><b>Trees on house or structures</b></div><div><small>Key issues</small><b>Removal limits, framing</b></div><div><small>Common after</small><b>Hurricanes, ice storms</b></div></div></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<h2>How tree fall claim appraisal in SC works</h2>
<p>Tree fall claim appraisal in SC is for losses where a tree or large limb struck the home or another covered structure and the insurer accepts the claim but underpays the repair. These claims are rarely simple. A single tree can damage roofing, rafters, ceilings, gutters, HVAC equipment, fences and contents, and water often follows through the opening.</p>
<p>Recent storms show how common this is. Hurricane Matthew toppled thousands of trees on Hilton Head Island and around Beaufort in 2016. Hurricane Helene in 2024 brought down huge numbers of trees across the Upstate and Aiken County, with long power outages. And the February 2014 ice storm left Aiken, the Midlands and the Pee Dee covered with broken pines and limbs.</p>
<h2>What a tree fall scope includes</h2>
<ul>
<li>Removing the tree from the structure, often with a crane, and any limits on that cost.</li>
<li>Emergency tarping or temporary bracing.</li>
<li>Roof decking, rafters, trusses and ceiling joists, not just shingles.</li>
<li>Interior damage from impact and from rain entering through the hole.</li>
<li>Gutters, siding, windows and HVAC equipment.</li>
<li>Fences, sheds, carports and other structures.</li>
</ul>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Removal limits</b>Many policies limit how much they pay for tree removal and debris, often a modest amount per tree and per loss, and some pay nothing for trees that fell without hitting a covered structure. The panel can price the work, but the policy sets the limit.</div>
<h2>Common tree fall estimate disputes</h2>
<h3>Hidden structural damage</h3>
<p>A tree can crack rafters or trusses several feet from the visible impact point. An estimate that only replaces visibly broken members may miss framing that needs to be sistered or replaced. An engineer’s report can help.</p>
<h3>Crane and access charges</h3>
<p>Removing a large oak from a roof in a tight Charleston lot or a wooded Greenville neighborhood may require a crane and specialized crew. Carriers sometimes price ordinary tree service instead.</p>
<h3>Ice storm damage</h3>
<p>Ice storms damage roofs, gutters and power service masts as well as trees. Burst pipes from extended power loss can add a water claim on top of the tree claim.</p>
<h3>Matching after a partial roof replacement</h3>
<p>Replacing only the impact area can leave a visibly patched roof, especially with discontinued shingles. How matching is handled depends on the policy.</p>
<h2>Documentation that helps a tree claim</h2>
<p>Photograph the tree on the structure before removal from multiple angles, including the root ball or break point. Keep invoices from the tree service, crane company and tarping crew, and save any arborist or engineer report. Our guide on <a href="/appraisal-guides/when-a-tree-falls-on-your-house/">what to do when a tree falls on your house</a> walks through the first steps.</p>
<h2>How a tree fall panel usually unfolds</h2>
<p>Most tree claims resolve best when both appraisers can see the framing. If the ceiling is open or the roof is tarped, we ask for a joint inspection of the attic or exposed structure so the scope of rafter and truss repair can be agreed before pricing begins. With structure settled, the remaining items are usually removal costs, matching and interior finishes. In wooded parts of Greenville, Spartanburg and Aiken, where several trees may have hit one home, we keep a separate line for each tree so removal limits are applied correctly.</p>
<h2>When appraisal isn’t the right tool for tree damage</h2>
<p>If the insurer says the tree was dead or diseased and the owner should have removed it, or that the damage is from neglect, the dispute is partly about coverage. A tree that fell on a neighbor’s property, or a neighbor’s tree that fell on yours, can raise liability questions that appraisal doesn’t address. And if the main gap is the removal sub-limit itself, appraisal can’t raise a limit written into the policy. A South Carolina attorney can advise on these issues.</p>

<h2>Questions about tree fall &amp; ice storm appraisal</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Does my policy pay to remove a tree that fell in my yard but missed the house?</summary><div class="a"><p>Often not, or only in limited cases. Many policies cover removal only when the tree damaged a covered structure, and they usually cap the amount.</p></div></details><details><summary>Who pays when a neighbor’s tree falls on my house?</summary><div class="a"><p>Generally your own policy responds to damage to your home. Whether the neighbor has any responsibility is a separate legal question for a South Carolina attorney.</p></div></details><details><summary>Can appraisal include hidden framing damage?</summary><div class="a"><p>Yes, if it is part of the covered loss. An engineer or contractor inspection that documents the hidden damage strengthens the estimate.</p></div></details></div>
